ence explicitly lists bismuth-containing formulations among agents that should not be used concomitantly with tetracyclines, aligning with the broader principle that metal ions interfere with tetracycline pharmacokinetics. [Data: Relationships (33708)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Metal ions broadly impair antibiotic absorption via chelation\",\n            \"explanation\": \"A recurring theme across multiple relationships is the negative impact of di- and trivalent metal ions—including Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, Fe²⁺, Al³⁺, and Bi³⁺—on the oral absorption of certain antibiotics. These metals form non-absorbable chelates with tetracyclines and possibly other drug classes, reducing their bioavailability. For instance, iron salts reduce tetracycline blood levels by 40–90% depending on the specific agent. This mechanistic insight explains why diverse substances such as milk (calcium), antacids (aluminum/magnesium), and bismuth preparations all share similar interaction profiles with tetracyclines. Clinically, this necessitates careful scheduling of antibiotic doses relative to intake of these substances—typically separating administration by 2–3 hours. [Data: Relationships (33661, 33708)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Community highlights critical pharmacokinetic drug-food interactions\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The entities and relationships in this community collectively emphasize the clinical significance of pharmacokinetic interactions between medications and dietary or co-administered agents. Unlike pharmacodynamic interactions (which affect drug action), these interactions primarily alter drug absorption, leading to reduced efficacy rather than direct toxicity. The documented cases—tetracyclines with milk or bismuth, fluoroquinolones with aluminum antacids—represent well-established, guideline-relevant interactions that healthcare providers must manage through patient education and dosing instructions. The presence of speci